INT. ICE-TUG COOLANT GALLERY - DRIFT SHIFT

Frost on every rivet. Amber work lights flicker down a corridor choked with pipe.

TESS OKONKWO (30s, burn scar across one palm, tool belt hanging low) drags a sled of frozen valve housings past a hatch while BRANDR HALVORSEN (50s, enormous, a thermos strapped to his chest like a medal) kneels at a leaking manifold, wrench in one fist, the other hand flat against the pipe as if feeling for a pulse. Tess kicks the sled brake, taps the gauge, and taps it again. Brandr doesn't look up.

TESS
(into her collar)
All hands on nine, gallery four is weeping coolant. Stay out of the aft ring.

No answer. Static, then nothing.

TESS (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Of course. Of course you're all asleep.

BRANDR
Hand me the forty.

She slides a wrench across the deck. He ignores it and keeps his palm on the pipe.

BRANDR (CONT'D)
It's not leaking. It's breathing.

TESS
Pipes don't breathe.

BRANDR
This one's learned.

The manifold gives a long, wet exhale. A white plume rolls across the ceiling. Tess backs into the sled, Brandr rocks onto his heels, and the work lights die one by one down the corridor, leaving only the glow of the gauge.

TESS
(to the dark, loud)
Everyone brace. Pressure drop, thirty seconds.

BRANDR
(low, to himself)
Easy, girl. Easy.

The gauge needle sags, hovers, and slowly climbs. The lights stutter back. Tess laughs once, short, and sits on the sled. Brandr unscrews his thermos and pours steaming coffee into the cap, which he holds out to her without looking.

TESS
You knew it would settle.

BRANDR
No.

She takes the cap anyway. Behind them, the pipe begins to knock, slow and rhythmic, like someone asking to be let in.

TESS
Brandr.

BRANDR
I heard it.

The knocking speeds up. They both stand.
